PRINTF(3)                      P32W Unix                       PRINTF(3)

NAME
       printf, fprintf, sprintf, snprintf - formatted output conversion

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int printf(const char *fmt, ...);
       int fprintf(FILE *f, const char *fmt, ...);
       int sprintf(char *s, const char *fmt, ...);
       int snprintf(char *s, size_t n, const char *fmt, ...);

DESCRIPTION
       These functions write their arguments, converted under the
       control of the format fmt: printf to stdout, fprintf to the
       stream f, sprintf into the string s, and snprintf into s but
       never more than n bytes, NUL included (with n of 0 it writes
       nothing). sprintf and snprintf end the string with a NUL.

       fmt is copied as it stands, except for conversions, which
       start with % and are, in order:

           % [flags] [width] [.precision] [h|l|ll] conversion

       Flags, in any order:
       -      left-justify within the width (pad on the right)
       0      pad numbers on the left with zeros instead of
              spaces; ignored with - or with a precision
       +      put + before a non-negative d or i
       space  put a space before a non-negative d or i (+ wins)
       #      o: start with 0; x, X: start a non-zero value with
              0x or 0X

       The width is the least number of characters to write; the
       output is padded with spaces (or zeros, by 0) on the left, or
       on the right with -. A * takes the width from the next int
       argument; a negative one means - and its size.

       The precision, after a dot (a dot alone is 0, and .* takes an
       int argument; a negative one counts as none), is for d, i,
       u, o, x and X the least number of digits, with leading zeros
       (a precision of 0 prints the value 0 as no digits at all);
       for s, the most characters to print.

       h and l are accepted and ignored: int and long are both 32
       bits. ll (%lld, %llu, %llx, %llo) takes a long long or an
       unsigned long long argument, 64 bits.

       Conversions:
       d, i   int, signed decimal
       u      unsigned decimal
       o      unsigned octal
       x, X   unsigned hexadecimal, with abcdef or ABCDEF
       c      int, as one character
       s      char *, a string (a null pointer prints (null))
       p      void *, as 0x and at least 8 hex digits
       %      a %

       Any other character after the % (and its flags) is printed as
       it stands, the % with it, and takes no argument.

       %e %E %f %F %g %G %a %A print a double (a float argument
       arrives as one), with the flags, width and precision as in C:
       the digits are the exact decimal value rounded half to even,
       so the output is the same as on any C library that rounds
       exactly (macOS's, glibc's), inf and nan included.

RETURN VALUE
       The number of characters written, not counting the NUL. For
       snprintf it is the number the whole output needed, so a
       result of n or more means the output was cut short.

EXAMPLES
       printf("%-8s %5d\n", name, count);    /* ken         42 */
       printf("%08x %#o\n", 48879, 8);       /* 0000beef 010 */
       printf("%.*s|\n", 3, "abcdef");       /* abc| */
       snprintf(path, sizeof path, "%s/%s", dir, name);

SEE ALSO
       vprintf(3), putc(3), stdio(3), stdarg(3)

BUGS
       There is no %n, no z, j or t length, and no positional
       arguments (%1$d).

       A write error does not change the result: check ferror(3).
       sprintf cannot know the size of s; use snprintf.

       printf, fprintf, sprintf and snprintf must be declared (by
       stdio.h) before they are called: cc refuses a call to an
       undeclared one, since a variadic function receives every
       argument on the stack.

       For reading formatted input, see scanf(3).
